Offset a curve past a re-entrant corner the way a line is.

What it fixes
  BRepOffsetAPI_MakeOffset with GeomAbs_Intersection fails on any closed
  outline in which a B-spline (or any curve that is not a line or a
  circle) meets its neighbour at a re-entrant corner, at every distance:
  the result wire does not close and Perform reports not done. Two
  things conspire, one in the medial axis and one in the parallels.

  Bisector_Bisec::Perform falls back to a half-line when
  Bisector_BisecCC finds no equidistant polygon, which it never does at
  a re-entrant corner between a curve and anything, because the points
  equidistant from both lie beyond the curve's end. The fallback's
  direction, -(first + second), bisects the salient side of the two
  tangents; at a re-entrant corner that half-line leaves the domain, so
  no parallel ever meets it and the medial axis runs to infinity from
  a closed contour. The analytic bissectrice of two lines (BisecAna)
  gets this case right, which is why a polyline with the same corner
  offsets. The fallback now turns round when the corner is re-entrant,
  read from the cross product of the tangents against the side asked.

  BRepFill_OffsetWire's MakeOffset extends a line's parallel to
  infinity and a circle's to a full turn under GeomAbs_Intersection, so
  the parallels of two edges meeting at a re-entrant corner reach the
  point where the join needs them to cross. A curve's parallel was
  Geom2d_OffsetCurve over the curve's own range, ending where the curve
  ends; with the bissectrice fixed it still missed the crossing by the
  width of the corner. It is now the parallel of the curve continued
  along its end tangent, a straight piece run at the curve's own end
  speed so the whole is one C1 B-spline, long enough for a corner that
  turns up to 2 atan(100) ~ 178.9 degrees — at the ends that need it.
  DivergingCorners reads the spine once: an end is extended only where
  the edge meets its neighbour at a corner the offset turns away from
  (re-entrant for an inward offset, salient for an outward one) or at a
  cusp. The straight extension and the straight fallback bissectrice
  agree exactly: a corner between a curve and a line lands where the
  same corner between two lines does, on the bisector of the tangents,
  distance / cos(turn / 2) from the corner.

What it does not change
  Salient corners, tangent joins (the pieces a curve is cut into at its
  curvature extrema, the seam of a closed curve), GeomAbs_Arc joins,
  open results (IsOpenResult trims nothing at the two ends) and outlines
  of lines and circles only, which never reach either path. Where
  BisecCC does find a polygon the fallback is not used. A parallel that
  is not extended is the object it was before, to the pole: extending
  every curve end, as a first version did, left the 3D approximation of
  a parallel that ran onto a straight piece eight times its poles (a
  fitted ring of 185 poles came back as 1480), because the parallel's
  speed jumps with the curvature at the join; only a parallel that
  reaches past its curve's end carries that join now.

Measured
  A 200 x 125 outline whose top-left edge meets a cubic Bezier at a
  re-entrant corner of 9.46 degrees offsets inward by 10 to a closed
  wire with its corner at (40.8276, 115), 10.034 = 10 / cos(4.73
  degrees) from the outline's corner at (40, 125); the same outline
  with that corner made salient offset before and after, and its
  Bezier's parallel keeps its 13 poles. A 960 x 215 outline with a
  ten-point interpolated spline meeting a straight edge at a 7 degree
  re-entrant corner, the first user part refused for this, offsets
  inward by 18. A closed 9-pole fit of a circle split in two offsets by
  2 to the same ten pieces of 6 or 7 poles as before. parcad measures
  every inset against its outline afterwards
  (vendor/opencascade/include/curve.hxx, ParcadInsetMeasure); that is
  where the geometry is held, not here.

